\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Degree parity anticommutes with the counting operator}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Keep #{N\ge 3} and the counting operator #{D} of
\ref{fdip-00OL} on the symmetric torus, including every
\vocabk{recovery site}{fdip-00PQ}. Let #{\omega} be the volume
operator of \ref{fdip-00OS}, with the largest index acting
first. Right multiplication is the Clifford product of that
theorem. The sign #{(-1)^{\deg}} anticommutes with #{D} over
any commutative unital ring. Left multiplication obeys
#{\omega\gamma_i=(-1)^{d-1}\gamma_i\omega}. Right
multiplication by #{\omega}, followed by left multiplication
by #{\omega}, multiplies a homogeneous element of degree #{p}
by #{(-1)^{d(d-1)/2}(-1)^{(d-1)p}}. This product equals
#{(-1)^{\deg}} for every degree exactly when #{4} divides
#{d}. In the other classes it is the identity, the opposite
degree sign, and multiplication by #{-1}, for
#{d=4m+1}, #{d=4m+2} and #{d=4m+3} respectively. On a vertex
function #{f}, with #{e_{\hat\imath}} the monomial on every
axis except #{i},
##{(\omega D+D\omega)f
=\sum_{i=1}^{d}(-1)^{i-1}
\bigl[(-1)^{d-1}\delta_i f+\delta_i^\dagger f\bigr]
e_{\hat\imath},}
##{(R_\omega D+DR_\omega)f
=\sum_{i=1}^{d}(-1)^{i-1}
\bigl(f(x+\varepsilon_i)+f(x-\varepsilon_i)-2f(x)\bigr)
e_{\hat\imath}.}
Both operators are nonzero. The case #{d=1} is
\ref{fdip-00PU}.}

\proof{
\p{The coboundary raises form degree by one and the adjoint
lowers it by one, so the degree sign anticommutes with each
summand of #{D}.}

\p{Conjugation by #{\gamma_i} fixes #{\gamma_i} and changes the
sign of each other generator, so
#{\gamma_i\omega\gamma_i=(-1)^{d-1}\omega}. Left
multiplication by #{\gamma_i} is the operator relation. Moving
each factor of the second copy of #{\omega} through the tail
before it gives #{\omega^2=(-1)^{d(d-1)/2}}. A monomial #{x}
of degree #{p} therefore satisfies
#{\omega x\omega=(-1)^{(d-1)p}x\omega^2}. For even #{p} and
for odd #{p}, the product equals #{(-1)^p} only when #{4}
divides #{d}.}

\p{Both multiplications commute with the differences. The left
anticommutator is the sum of #{\{\omega,\epsilon_i\}} on
#{\delta_i} and of #{\{\omega,\iota_i\}} on
#{\delta_i^\dagger}, and the right anticommutator has the same
shape. The volume sends #{1} to the top monomial, and
#{\epsilon_i} kills that monomial. Contraction along axis
#{i} multiplies the complementary monomial by #{(-1)^{i-1}}.
Hence #{\omega(e_i)=(-1)^{d-1}(-1)^{i-1}e_{\hat\imath}}, which
is the forward coefficient, and the backward coefficient is
#{(-1)^{i-1}e_{\hat\imath}}. On the right, #{e_i\omega} equals
that contraction: #{e_i} passes #{i-1} factors before its
square cancels. Adding the two differences produces the second
difference.}

\p{The vertices #{x}, #{x+\varepsilon_i} and
#{x-\varepsilon_i} are distinct. The function equal to #{1} at
one vertex and #{0} elsewhere has forward difference #{1} and
backward difference #{0} at the backward neighbor. Both
displayed coefficients equal a unit there.}
}
